- *(a) "z,* **+p** *(~"3 "a(z,)* **+p** *a((~)."Statedifferently,*

$$\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{z}_n) = \mathbf{a}(\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \mathbf{z}_n)$$

*provided the* plim *exists.* 

- *(b) "z,* **+d** *z7'3 "a(z,)* **+d** *a@)."*

An immediate implication of Lemma 2.3(a) is that the usual arithmetic operations preserve convergence in probability. For example:

$$\text{"}x_n \rightarrow \beta, y_n \rightarrow \gamma\text{"} \Rightarrow \text{"}x_n + y_n \rightarrow \beta + \gamma\text{"}$$

$$\text{"}x_n \xrightarrow{P} \beta, y_n \xrightarrow{P} \gamma\text{"} \Rightarrow \text{"}x_n y_n \xrightarrow{P} \beta \gamma\text{"}$$

$$x_n \rightarrow \beta$$
,  $y_n \rightarrow \gamma$   $\Rightarrow$  " $x_n/y_n \rightarrow \beta/\gamma$ " provided that  $\gamma \neq 0$ .

$$\mathbf{Y}_n \rightarrow \Gamma$$
  $\Rightarrow$   $\mathbf{Y}_n^{-1} \rightarrow \Gamma^{-1}$ , provided that  $\Gamma$  is invertible.

