*where* **T** *is the sample cross-moment matrix of transformed residuals associated with the fixed-effects estimator:* 

$$(\widehat{\mathbf{V}}_{(M \times M)}) \equiv \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_i - \tilde{\mathbf{F}}_i \widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{\text{FE}})(\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_i - \tilde{\mathbf{F}}_i \widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{\text{FE}})'. \quad (5.2.9)$$

The only nontrivial part of the proof is to show:

- (1) E(QFi) is nonsingular (and hence invertible),
- (2) E(F:Qqi) = **0** (which is needed for consistency), and
- (3) E(QijiijjFi) (which is the Avar of x:=, Fiji) = E[Q E(ijiijj)Fi].

The rest of the proof is an easy application of Kolmogorov's LLN and the Lindeberg-Levy CLT.

Proving that (5.1.15) implies (1) is left as an optional analytical exercise.

To prove (2), use formula (4.6.16b) and rewrite the expectation **as** 

$$\begin{aligned} E(\mathbf{F}'_i \mathbf{Q} \boldsymbol{\eta}_i) &= E\left(\sum_{m=1}^M \sum_{h=1}^M q_{mh} \mathbf{f}_{im} \cdot \boldsymbol{\eta}_{ih}\right) \quad (q_{mh} \equiv (m, h) \text{ element of } \mathbf{Q}) \\ &= \sum_{m=1}^M \sum_{h=1}^M q_{mh} E(\mathbf{f}_{im} \cdot \boldsymbol{\eta}_{ih}). \end{aligned} \quad (5.2.10)$$

This is zero by the orthogonality conditions (5.2.6).

Proof of (3) is as follows. First note that

$$E(\tilde{\mathbf{F}}'_i \tilde{\mathbf{\eta}}_i \tilde{\mathbf{\eta}}'_i \tilde{\mathbf{F}}_i) = E[\tilde{\mathbf{F}}'_i E(\tilde{\mathbf{\eta}}_i \tilde{\mathbf{\eta}}'_i \mid \mathbf{x}_i) \tilde{\mathbf{F}}_i].$$

(This follows by the Law of Total Expectations and by the fact that x; [= union of (zil, . . . , ziM)] includes the elements of Fi and F; is a function of Fi.) But ~(ij, iji I xi) equals the unconditional mean E(iji ijj) because

$$\begin{aligned} E(\tilde{\eta}_i \tilde{\eta}_i' \mid \mathbf{x}_i) &= E(\tilde{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}_i \tilde{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}_i' \mid \mathbf{x}_i) \quad (\text{since } \tilde{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}_i \equiv \mathbf{Q} \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_i = \mathbf{Q}(\mathbf{1}_M \cdot \boldsymbol{\alpha}_i + \boldsymbol{\eta}_i) = \mathbf{Q} \boldsymbol{\eta}_i = \tilde{\boldsymbol{\eta}}_i) \\ &= \mathbf{Q} E(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_i \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_i' \mid \mathbf{x}_i) \mathbf{Q} \\ &= \mathbf{Q} E(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_i \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_i') \mathbf{Q} \quad (\text{by (5.1.5)}) \\ &= E(\tilde{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}_i \tilde{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}_i') \\ &= E(\tilde{\eta}_i \tilde{\eta}_i') \quad (\text{since } \tilde{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}_i = \tilde{\boldsymbol{\eta}}_i). \end{aligned} \tag{5.2.11}$$

