The MA(m) process in (6.1.6) is said to be one-sided because the moving average does not include future values of **E,.** It is a special case of a linear process, which is defined to be two-sided moving averages of the form:

$$y_t = \mu + \sum_{j=-\infty}^{\infty} \psi_j \varepsilon_{t-j} \quad \text{with} \quad \sum_{j=-\infty}^{\infty} |\psi_j| < \infty.$$

The proposition (and all the results in this section) can easily be extended to general linear processes. We nevertheless focus on one-sided moving averages, only because two-sided moving averages are very rarely encountered in economics.

