MA(co) processes if the coefficients are absolutely summable, as the following result assures us.

## Proposition 6.6 (Autocovariance-generating function for filtered processes):

*Let* {E,) *be white noise and let* +(L) = + +l L + +2~2 + . . . *be an absolutely summable filter (i.e., with absolutely summable). Then the autocovan'ancegenerating function of the* MA(m) *process* {y,) *where* y, = **p** + +(L)E~ *is* 

$$g_Y(z) = \sigma^2 \psi(z) \psi(z^{-1}). \quad (6.2.19)$$

*More generally, let* {x, ) *be covariance-stationary with absolutely summable auto~ov~ances and* **gx** (z) *be the autocovariance-generating function of* {x,]. *The auto~ov~ance-genera ting function of the filtered series* { y,) *where* y, = h (L)x, *(with absolutely summable* (h,]) *is* 

$$g_Y(z) = h(z) g_X(z) h(z^{-1}). \quad (6.2.20)$$

We will not prove this result; see, e.g., Theorem 4.3.1 of Fuller (1996).

Because AR(p) and ARMA(p, q) processes have the MA(m) representations, their autocovariance-generating functions can be derived easily from (6.2.19). Since +(L) = l/@(L) for AR(p), and +(L) = B(L)/\$(L) for ARMA(p, q), we have

$$\text{AR}(p): g_Y(z) = \sigma^2 \frac{1}{\phi(z) \phi(z^{-1})}, \quad (6.2.21)$$

$$\text{ARMA}(p, q): g_Y(z) = \sigma^2 \frac{\theta(z) \theta(z^{-1})}{\phi(z) \phi(z^{-1})}. \quad (6.2.22)$$

