## **Linear and Nonlinear GMM**

In Chapter 3, we applied GMM to linear equations. The linear equation to be estimated was

$$y_t = \mathbf{z}_t'\boldsymbol{\theta}_0 + \varepsilon_t. \quad (7.1.21)$$

With x, as the vector of instruments, the orthogonality (zero-mean) conditions were

$$\mathbf{E}[\mathbf{x}_t \cdot (y_t - \mathbf{z}_t'\boldsymbol{\theta}_0)] = \mathbf{0}. \quad (7.1.22)$$

The correctly specified model here is a set of ergodic stationary processes w, = (y,, zi, xi)' such that these zero-mean conditions hold for 80 in O. The linear GMM estimator of 80 is a GMM estimator with the g function in the GMM objective function (7.1.3) given by

$$\mathbf{g}(\mathbf{w}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}) \equiv \mathbf{x}_t \cdot (y_t - \mathbf{z}_t'\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \mathbf{x}_t \cdot y_t - \mathbf{x}_t'\mathbf{z}_t'\boldsymbol{\theta}. \quad (7.1.23)$$

GMM can be readily applied to nonlinear equations. Suppose that the estimation equation is nonlinear and also implicit in y, :

$$a(y_t, \mathbf{z}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0) = \varepsilon_t. \quad (7.1.24)$$

Just set g(w, ; 8) = x, *.a* (y, , z, ; *8).* This estimator is called a generalized nonlinear instrumental variables e~timator.~ It is still a special case of GMM because the g function here can be written as a product of the vector of instruments and the error term. The properties of GMM estimators to be developed in this chapter do not rely on this special structure.

As an example of nonlinear GMM, consider the nonlinear Euler equation of Hansen and Singleton (1982).

Example 7.5 (Nonlinear consumption Euler equation): The Euler equation for the household optimization problem, standard in macroeconomics, is

$$E \left[ R_{i+1} \frac{\beta_0 u'(c_{i+1})}{u'(c_i)} \mid I_t \right] = 1, \quad (7.1.25)$$

where R,,, is the gross ex-post rate of return (1 plus the rate of return) from the asset in question, *c,* is consumption, Po is the discounting factor, u'(c) is the marginal utility, and I, is the information available at date *r.* Assume for

<sup>3~</sup>t is more general than the usual nonlinear instrumental variables estimator because conditional homoskedasticity is not assumed.

<!-- page 471 -->

the utility function that u (c) = ~'-~o/(l - uo). Then ul(c) = C-~O and the Euler equation becomes

$$E\left[a\left(\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_t}, R_{t+1}; \alpha_0, \beta_0\right) \mid I_t\right] = 0$$

$$\text{with } a\left(\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_t}, R_{t+1}; \alpha_0, \beta_0\right) \equiv R_{t+1} \cdot \beta_0 \cdot \left(\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_t}\right)^{-\alpha_0} - 1. \quad (7.1.26)$$

If x, is a vector of variables whose values are known at date t, then x, **E** I, and it follows from (7.1.26) that

$$E \left[ \mathbf{x}_t \cdot a \left( \frac{c_{t+1}}{c_t}, R_{t+1}; \alpha_0, \beta_0 \right) \mid I_t \right] = \mathbf{0}. \quad (7.1.27)$$

Taking the unconditional expectation of both sides and using the Law of Total Expectations (that E[E(x I I,)] = E(x)), we obtain the orthogonality (zeromean) conditions :E[g(w,; Bo)] = **0** where

$$\mathbf{g}(\mathbf{w}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0) = \mathbf{x}_t \cdot \mathbf{a}\left(\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_t}, R_{t+1}; \alpha_0, \beta_0\right) \quad \text{with} \quad \mathbf{w}_t = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{x}_t \\ c_{t+1}/c_t \\ R_{t+1} \end{bmatrix}, \quad \boldsymbol{\theta}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} \beta_0 \\ \alpha_0 \end{bmatrix}. \quad (7.1.28)$$

