ML

For ML, the role played by (7.2.8) for NLS is played by the **Kullback-Leibler information inequality.** Here we examine identification for conditional ML (doing the same for unconditional or joint ML is more straightforward). The hypothetical conditional density f (y, I x,; O), being a function of (y,, x,), is a random variable for any 8. The expected value of the random variable log f (y, I x,; 8) can be written as8

$$E[\log f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta})] = \int \log f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}) f(y_t, \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0, \boldsymbol{\psi}_0) dy_t d\mathbf{x}_t. \quad (7.2.11)$$

Note well that the expected value is with respect to the *true* joint density f (y,, x,; 00, 96,). The Kullback-Leibler information inequality about density functions (adapted to conditional densities) states that

$$\begin{cases} \text{E}[\log f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta})] < \text{E}[\log f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0)] & \text{if } f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}) \neq f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0), \\ \text{E}[\log f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta})] = \text{E}[\log f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0)] & \text{if } f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}) = f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0) \end{cases} \quad (7.2.12)$$

(provided, of course, that E[log f (y, I x,; O)] exists and is finite). (See Analytical Exercise 1 for derivation.) It immediately follows that the identification condition (that E[log f (y, I x,; 8)] be maximized uniquely at 80) is satisfied if

conditional density identification: 
$$f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \theta) \neq f(y_t \mid \mathbf{x}_t; \theta_0)$$
  
 for all  $\theta \neq \theta_0$ . (7.2.13)

