# **Expressing the Sampling Error in a Common Format**

To prepare for the next section's discussion, it is useful to develop a slightly different proof of asymptotic normality. We have used the Mean Value Theorem to derive the mean value expansion for the sampling error, which is (7.3.7) for M-estimators and (7.3.32) for GMM. We show in this subsection that they can be written in a common format, (7.3.43) below.

Consider M-estimators first. Noting that = 1 C:=l s(wt; eo), the mean value expansion (7.3.7) can be written as

$$\sqrt{n} (\hat{\theta} - \theta_0) = - \left[ \frac{1}{n} \sum_{t=1}^n \mathbf{H}(\mathbf{w}_t; \bar{\theta}) \right]^{-1} \sqrt{n} \frac{\partial Q_n(\theta_0)}{\partial \theta}. \quad (7.3.40)$$

By condition (4) of Proposition 7.8, C:=, H(wt; **e)** converges in probability to some p x p symmetric matrix **q** given by

$$\mathbf{\Psi} = \mathbf{E}[\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{w}_t; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0)]. \quad (7.3.41)$$

So rewrite (7.3.40) as

$$\begin{aligned} \sqrt{n}(\hat{\theta} - \theta_0) &= -\Psi^{-1}\sqrt{n} \frac{\partial Q_n(\theta_0)}{\partial \theta} \\ &\quad - \left\{ \left[ \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf{H}(\mathbf{w}_i; \bar{\theta}) \right]^{-1} - \Psi^{-1} \right\} \sqrt{n} \frac{\partial Q_n(\theta_0)}{\partial \theta}. \quad (7.3.42) \end{aligned}$$

By construction, the term in braces converges in probability to zero. By condition 1 n (3) of Proposition 7.8. fi (= 7 Et=, s(w,; 00)) converges to a random <sup>J</sup> variable. So the last term, which is the product of the term in braces and fi w. converges to zero in probability by Lemma 2.4(b). This fact can be written compactly as a Taylor expansion:16

$$\sqrt{n}(\hat{\theta} - \theta_0) = -\frac{\Psi^{-1}}{(p \times p)} \sqrt{n} \frac{\partial Q_n(\theta_0)}{\partial \theta} + o_p, \quad (7.3.43)$$

where the term *"o,"* means "some random variable that converges to zero in probability."17 The exact expression for the *o,* term will depend on the context. Here, it equals the last term in (7.3.42). As you will see, all that matters for our argument

**I61t is apt to call this equation a Taylor expansion rather than a mean value expansion because the matrix that multiplies fi is evaluated at** *0,* **rather than at 6 as in the mean value expansion.** 

