#### **ANSWERS TO SELECTED QUESTIONS**

**2a.** Since f (y I **x;** 8) is a hypothetical density, its integral is unity:

$$\int f(y \mid \mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) dy = 1. \quad (1)$$

This is an identity, valid for any 8 **E** O. Differentiating both sides of this identity with respect to 8, we obtain

$$\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \int f(y \mid \mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) dy = \mathbf{0}_{(p \times 1)}. \quad (2)$$

If the order of differentiation and integration can be interchanged, then

$$\frac{\partial}{\partial \boldsymbol{\theta}} \int f(y \mid \mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) dy = \int \frac{\partial}{\partial \boldsymbol{\theta}} f(y \mid \mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) dy. \quad (3)$$

But by the definition of the score, s(w; 8) f (y I x; 0) = \$ f **(y** I x; 8). Substituting this into (3), we obtain

$$\int \mathbf{s}(\mathbf{w}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) f(y \mid \mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) dy = \mathbf{0}_{(p \times 1)}. \quad (4)$$

This holds for any 0 **E** O, in particular, for 80. Setting 8 = 80, we obtain

$$\int \mathbf{s}(\mathbf{w}; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0) f(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0) d\mathbf{y} = E[\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{w}; \boldsymbol{\theta}_0) \mid \mathbf{x}] = \mathbf{0}_{(p \times 1)}. \quad (5)$$

Then, by the Law of Total Expectations, we obtain the desired result.

