Figure 10.1 (a): Log Income and Consumption

# **Linear Vector I(0) and 1(1) Processes**

Our definition of vector I(0) and 1(1) processes follows Hamilton (1994) and Johansen (1995). To introduce the multivariate extension of a zero-mean linear

<!-- page 641 -->

univariate I(0) process, consider a zero-mean n-dimensional VMA (vector moving average) process:

$$\mathbf{u}_t = \Psi(L) \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_t, \quad \Psi(L) \equiv \mathbf{I}_n + \Psi_1 L + \Psi_2 L^2 + \dots, \quad (10.1.1)$$

where E, is i.i.d. with

$$\mathbf{E}(\mathbf{e}_t) = \mathbf{0}, \quad \mathbf{E}(\mathbf{e}_t'_t) = \frac{\mathbf{\Omega}}{(n \times n)}, \quad \mathbf{\Omega} \text{ positive definite.} \quad (10.1.2)$$

(The error process E, could be a vector Martingale Difference Sequence satisfying certain properties, but we will not allow for this degree of generality.) As in the univariate case, we impose two restrictions. The first is one-summability:'

$$\{\Psi_j\}$$
 is one-summable. (10.1.3)

Since {qj} is absolutely summable when one-summability holds, the multivariate version of Proposition 6.l(d) means that u, is (strictly) stationary and ergodic. The second restriction on the zero-mean VMA process is

$$\Psi(1) \neq \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ (n \times n) ) \end{pmatrix} \quad (n \times n \text{ matrix of zeros}). \quad (10.1.4)$$

That is, at least one element of the n x n matrix q(1) is not zero. This is a natural multivariate extension of condition (9.2.3b) in the definition of univariate I(0) processes on page 564.

A (linear) zero-mean n-dimensional vector **I(0)** process or a (linear) zeromean **I(0)** system is a VMA process satisfying (10.1.1)-(10.1.4). Given a zeromean I(0) system u,, a (linear) vector **I(0)** process or a (linear) **I(0)** system can be written as

$$\delta + \mathbf{u}_t,$$

where 6 is an n-dimensional vector representing the mean of the stationary process. Using the formula (6.5.6) with (6.3.15), the long-run covariance matrix of the I(0) system is

$$\Psi(1)\Omega\Psi(1)'.$$
 (10.1.5)

Since **8** is positive definite and q(1) satisfies (10.1.4), at least one of the diagonal

**<sup>&#</sup>x27;A** sequence of matrices **{Qj** J is said to be one-summable if **{qj,ke]** is one-summable (i.e., **jIqj,ke I** < CO) for all k, e = 1,2, . . . . **n,** where **qj,ke** is the **(k, t)** element of the n x n matrix **Q,.** 

